Steam Enhanced: no :has() above a compound without a class, id or attribute (21 rules, 11 files).

Why. Blink keeps one invalidation set for the :has() pseudo-class per document: everything that can
sit under a :has() anchor, from every rule in the window. One rule with a :has() above a compound
that has nothing to match on (`A:has(B) > *`, `A:has(B) > :not(.c)`, `A:has(B) + *`) makes that set
"the whole subtree", for every anchor. And <body> is an anchor anyway (with every theme :has()
removed, Steam's own rules still schedule :has() invalidations on it). So in the main
window each DOM change that could touch a :has() argument (most React commits on a game page, their
own data-se-* attributes) recalculated the style of the whole window. All the selectors below live
in files the main window loads (settings, dialogs, menus, Extendium...), and any one of them is
enough: with all but the settings ones removed, or all but Extendium's and the friends list's,
the cost stayed as it was. Measured in the main window, window occluded, so its frames driven over CDP
(a forced style and layout every 16 ms, ResizeObserver delivered after it), Home -> CS2's game
page, 3 s after the route change (trace, StyleInvalidationTracking): whole-<body> recalculations
20 -> 0, elements restyled 37,880 -> 8,419, style recalculation 1,710 -> 1,229 ms (one run); with
0004 too, Performance.getMetrics mean of 3 runs: style 1,649 -> 1,153 ms, layout 560 -> 351 ms,
main thread 2,474 -> 1,820 ms.

What. Each selector says the same without a :has() above a featureless compound:
  - a parent "having" a child that is the subject's sibling -> the sibling combinators
    (`P:has(> X) > :not(X)` -> `P > X ~ :not(X), P > :not(X):has(~ X)`), and for a descendant
    anywhere in the parent: the child itself, a following sibling, a preceding one (dialogs.css);
  - a :has() the subject already implies is dropped (`L:has(svg, img) svg` -> `L svg`;
    `:has(> * > .row) > :has(> .row)` -> `* > :has(> .row)`; `A:has(> O > :nth-child(2)) > O >
    :not(:last-child)` -> `A > O > :not(:last-child)`);
  - selector weight is kept as it was: `:not(.se-none)` / `:not(se-none)` (no element has that class
    or tag) stand in for the class or type a dropped :has() counted, :where() hides added parts.
Three rewrites rest on Steam's markup as its code draws it now (sp.js):
  - controls.css, FieldLabelRow's children other than FieldLabel: Steam draws only FieldLabel in
    it, so the rule matches nothing either way; the row condition (FieldChildrenWithIcon as a direct
    child) is dropped;
  - game-properties.css, :last-child: FieldChildrenWithIcon holds one FieldChildrenInner;
  - game-properties-install.css: a shortcut's Properties page has ShortcutRow as DialogBody's
    first child, before the fields.
extendium.css: an off extension's card dims .extension-main as a whole instead of each of its
children (it draws only padding: the same picture). Computed styles of every element on the game
page, Home and Downloads are identical before and after (DOMSnapshot, all properties); the other
rules are in windows not checked here (settings, game properties, menus, dialogs).

